you're sitting inside goldman sachs they  bought your business you become the head  of product at marcus it's like  do people think that they can compete  against the fintechs is it like oh man  this is an uphill battle what's like the  the mentality inside of these kind of  incumbent financial institutions at the  intersection of their core business  versus like more of a technology-enabled  uh market that seems to be you know very  very popular with uh with investors and  also users  you gotta hand it to goldman sachs you  know not having a meaningful retail  business and moving headlong into  a fintech bank startup inside goldman  sachs  was a bold move  for for goldman and it's really paid off  um  you know the the deposits  uh in terms of aum the number of  customers the apple card their  distribution deals with gm they've  really um kind of knocked the cover off  the ball and you know i being an  entrepreneur always sort of thought  about large  uh companies as these sort of slow  uh  moving uh behemoths that didn't really  innovate very much  but goldman sachs with marcus really has  done quite a good job  yeah when you think about actually uh  building that how much of it is hey we  need to go compete out outside with  these like fintech companies versus  let's think about what we uniquely can  bring to the market and just layer  technology on top of our unique  advantage like is it competing and being  forced by the external forces or is it  more something being driven by the  internal uh advantages  yeah it's a bit of both i mean one of  the reasons they acquired my business  clarity money is that we had developed a  set of technologies that  gave consumers a very useful way to  manage their money and they recognized  that that tool coupled with the  credibility of goldman sachs  layered on top of the  uh the offer that they made available to  retail uh  consumers both in terms of the interest  rate on deposits and the rate they  charged to borrow on the lending side of  the business  was was very compelling uh and that  combination turned out to be right uh  you know if you if you use the product  today uh marcus it's very slick uh it's  very intuitive uh it delivers a lot of  value independent of banking or or you  know borrowing or lending excuse me uh a  lot of personal financial management  tools that uh retail investors and  and bank customers find very useful  yeah when you're sitting there obviously  uh marcus has had incredible success uh  across the industry people look at it as  kind of the the light on uh on the hill  right in terms of how an incumbent  business can go and play in this kind of  fintech space uh at some point you  decide hey maybe i'm gonna leave uh is  it driven by like all right i've kind of  squeezed as much economics out of this  place let me go do it again and  hopefully they'll buy the next one like  what what's the general thought process  as to when you as an entrepreneur know  i should stay and i should keep building  you know inside of a place like goldman  versus like okay now i think it's time  to go leave and start another business  yeah well i'm a serial entrepreneur i  started five companies uh clarity money  which goldman bought was my last domain  money is my current business  i left goldman because i saw a real  opportunity um you know i spent three  years inside the bowels of our banking  system  messing around with the roughly hundred  ach networks that make up our  global payment system  and was shocked by how inefficient they  are i understood that in an intellectual  level but i really experienced it at an  operational level uh when i was  working on marcus and that really led me  down the path of exploring blockchains  more carefully  and you know the average ach net  transaction takes two days and costs two  bucks to settle and while to the  consumer it feels relatively  straightforward to buy your latte and  swipe your credit card and move move on  with your day  the back end of that process is  incredibly inefficient there are  blockchains anthony out there as you  know that can do the same transaction  in two seconds for a 40th of a penny and  if you look at the  the inefficiencies  in the current system and the relative  gain opportunities from the technologies  emerging out of blockchains it doesn't  take long to figure out what a big  opportunity is out there  and and the other thing that really  struck  a chord for me was just the engineering  activity if you look for a startup  focused on banking technologies you  can't find one that isn't doing stuff in  blockchains it's just there's no energy  there uh outside of the crypto ecosystem  and so the engineering community which  is really the the driving force of  innovation and where we're headed uh uh  is has voted with their feet and and  what they voted on as blockchains and so  to me that represented a very exciting  opportunity and why why i started domain  with roughly 30 of my colleagues from  goldman who joined me when i left the  firm yeah it's fascinating to kind of  think through the psychology of it uh  you obviously saw the problems do you do  a bunch of market research or like walk  me through as a serial entrepreneur like  how do you go about evaluating okay i've  got 20 good ideas but are they actually  great ideas is it worth starting a  company what's the actual addressable  market like what does that process look  like for you yeah i like that question  because i have a good answer for it so  we did about 10 000  consumer uh surveys where we really got  into the head of different personas  uh around investing in crypto investing  in in this new asset class  and what's really clear is that you have  you know a very powerful uh early  adopter crowd that makes up you know in  large part you know maybe your audience  anthony but there's a much larger  segment of the population out there that  recognizes that you know crypto is is  here to stay it's a big deal they don't  really understand it they want to make  it part of their uh portfolio don't  really have a good way to do that other  than guess  and guessing is not a great investment  strategy and so we saw an opportunity to  come to market with a set of portfolios  that we've curated and managed uh  through our investment team that spends  every day thinking about which of the  blockchain technologies and  opportunities are the most interesting  from an investment standpoint and we've  come to market with you know managed  portfolios you press a button and we'll  buy a basket of assets for you and we'll  manage those assets um over time  that's a  you know a pretty compelling value  proposition from my standpoint i'm  obviously biased because i built the  business around it but to answer your  question you know i i see very clearly a  real disconnect between these  technologies and consumers experience  let me explain what i mean you know the  average consumer can touch amazon touch  disney touch tesla but it's very tough  for the average investor out there to  really get their head around what is  solana what is cardano what is the  theory what's polygon what are these  technologies and which ones are likely  to win and so i think there's a real  need in the market for a solution like  the one we offer which is we have an  investment team we stay very close to  the developer community we really watch  these protocols carefully we look at uh  transaction activity economic utility uh  relative to alternatives and we make our  bets based on those those parameters hey  you did you like this video great we  make five of them a day and post them  here on this channel make sure you  subscribe like the video and see you  next time

